<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.14.1: Evaluating Quadratic Functions in the Real World</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01989</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.14.1: Evaluating Quadratic Functions in the Real World</md:title>
    <md:uuid>55c1d375-5819-43ef-bbc9-5559f1d23eed</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <para id="para-00001">The height, in inches, of a frog's jump is modeled by the equation \(h(t)=60t-75t^2\), where the time \(t\), after the frog jumps, is measured in seconds.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">
          <media alt="close-up of a frog on plant stem">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_16_02_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
        <item>For \(h(t)=60t-75t^2\):</item>
      </list>
      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00002">
        <title>Warm Up Activity</title>
        <exercise id="_67eec057-f46f-4f3c-887c-aad2d0a7c8a2" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            
            <para id="para-00003">Find \(h(0)\).</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00004">Correct! 0</para>
            <para id="para-00005">The correct answer is 0.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_09870dcf-34f0-4827-b8a6-03b437b7fe10" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            
            <para id="para-00006">Find \(h(0.8)\).</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00007">Correct! 0</para>
            <para id="para-00008">The correct answer is 0.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00009">What do these values of \(h(0)\) and \(h(0.8)\) mean in terms of the frog's jump?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00010">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00011">They mean that the frog is on the ground when \(t=0\) (before jumping) and when \(t=0.8\) (when the frog lands on the ground).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00003">
        <exercise id="_1fe24a64-efa0-46d4-9188-efa86198aa89" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            
            <para id="para-00012">After it jumped, how much time passed, in seconds, before the frog reached its maximum height?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00013">Correct! 0.4 seconds</para>
            <para id="para-00014">The correct answer is 0.4 seconds.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00015">Explain how you know this is the time the frog reached the maximum height.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00016">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00017">The frog reached its maximum height at \(t=0.4\), as that is the halfway point of the jump. If we graph the function, the vertex of the graph will have a horizontal coordinate (\(t\)-coordinate) of 0.4.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
  </content></document>